  <h2>What a state insurance department is</h2>
  <p>
    Every U.S. state, the District of Columbia, and the five U.S. territories has a Department of
    Insurance (DOI) — sometimes called an Insurance Division, Insurance Administration, or Office
    of the Insurance Commissioner instead. It is a free public regulator, not a private company, and
    it exists specifically to answer consumer questions, investigate complaints, and enforce
    insurance law within that state or territory.
  </p>

  <h2>What it can help with</h2>
  <p>
    Per the NAIC's own consumer guidance, a DOI can help when you think your claim may be wrongly
    delayed, denied, or underpaid; think a company or agent treated you unfairly or you suspect
    fraud; have general questions about coverage, billing, cancellations, or non-renewals; need to
    check whether a company or agent is licensed; or need help after a natural disaster. If an
    insurer breaks the rules, the DOI can take enforcement action.
  </p>

  <h2>What it does not do</h2>
  <p>
    The same NAIC guidance is explicit about the limits: a DOI does not set insurance prices (it
    reviews or approves rates as required by state law), does not act as your personal legal counsel
    in court, and cannot force a company to sell you a policy. It can investigate unfair practices and
    explain your options — it is not a substitute for a licensed attorney if your situation needs one.
    See <a href="/guides/claim-process/when-to-consider-an-independent-appraisal-or-attorney/">When to
    Consider an Independent Appraisal or Attorney</a> for that separate decision.
  </p>

  <h2>How to find your state's DOI and file a complaint</h2>
  <p>
    Rather than publishing a 50-state list of phone numbers and mailing addresses on this page — a
    list that would inevitably go stale between reviews — this guide links directly to the source
    that insurance regulators themselves maintain: the NAIC's
    <a href="https://content.naic.org/state-insurance-departments" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Insurance Departments contact directory</a>.
    Select your state or territory there for that department's current phone number, complaint form,
    and website.
  </p>
  <div class="limits-note">
    <strong>This site does not operate that directory.</strong> It is a tool run by the National
    Association of Insurance Commissioners, the standard-setting organization governed by the chief
    insurance regulators of the 50 states, D.C., and five U.S. territories — not by
    FairClaimCalculator. Verify you're on <code>content.naic.org</code> before entering any
    information.
  </div>

  <h2>What to gather before you file</h2>
  <p>Per NAIC's own guidance on filing a complaint, before you contact your DOI:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Be ready to complete a paper or online form with your name, address, type of insurance, and the reason for the complaint.</li>
    <li>Gather supporting documents and photographs, including email correspondence and a log of phone calls with your agent or company.</li>
    <li>Write a detailed, factual account of what happened and the reason for your complaint.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The <a href="/calculators/claim-letter-builder/">Claim Letter &amp; Evidence Packet Builder</a>
    and the <a href="/guides/claim-process/auto-claim-evidence-checklist/">Auto Claim Evidence
    Checklist</a> can help you organize this material before you file.
  </p>

  <h2>Researching a company's complaint history</h2>
  <p>
    The NAIC also compiles closed, confirmed complaint data reported by state insurance departments,
    searchable by state, company, and insurance type for the past three years, through its Consumer
    Insurance Search tool. Per NAIC's own guidance, a complaint index should never be the only factor
    in a decision — compare complaints alongside a company's financial condition and how long it has
    been in business, since fluctuations in market-wide premiums or complaints can move a single
    company's index even without a change in that company's own behavior.
  </p>

  <h2>What this guide cannot tell you</h2>
  <p>
    It cannot tell you whether your specific complaint will succeed, how long your state's DOI will
    take to respond, or what enforcement action, if any, it will take. Complaint processes, contact
    information, and response times vary by state and change over time — always use the current
    contact details from the NAIC directory linked above rather than any cached or older version of
    this page.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
